{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgN5T6TmujPrqjBVvGEP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I found and fixed two real bugs plus several protocol gaps. All 39 tests now pass. Against the original `server.py` / `tools.py`, the new tests fail (16 failures, 27 errors), so they do catch these problems. Nothing is committed.\n\n## Bugs fixed\n\n1. **`get_note` fails in production.** DynamoDB returns every number as `Decimal`, including nested ones. `_coerce` only converted the top-level `word_count`, so `stats.views`, `rating` and `revisions` stayed Decimal. Every `get_note` call against `DynamoStore` returned `isError: \"Object of type Decimal is not JSON serializable\"`. The existing tests only used `FakeStore`, so they never saw this. `tools._coerce` now converts Decimals at any depth: whole numbers become `int` and the rest become `float`, matching the declared `outputSchema`.\n2. **The server answered notifications.** Every client sends `notifications/initialized` right after `initialize`, and the server replied with a `-32601` error. JSON-RPC requires that messages without an `id` get no reply. `handle()` now returns `\"\"` for them. I added a line to the README saying the HTTP layer should send `202 Accepted` with no body in that case.\n\n## Protocol hardening in `server.py`\n- Malformed JSON now gets a `-32700` parse error. Before, `json.loads` raised out of `handle()`.\n- Bodies that aren't a request object get `-32600` invalid request: arrays, scalars, a missing `jsonrpc`/`method`, or a non-string `method`. These used to crash.\n- An unknown tool, missing or wrongly typed arguments, or non-object `params`/`arguments` now get `-32602` invalid params, as the MCP spec requires. `null` params and arguments are treated as `{}`.\n- Added `ping`, which returns `{}`.\n- Tool error text no longer has the extra quotes that `str(KeyError)` adds: it now reads `no note 'x'`.\n\n## Tests\n- **`tests/test_server.py`** sends raw JSON through `handle()` the way a client does. Every test runs against **both** `FakeStore` and `DynamoStore`. It covers:\n  - the handshake, including the follow-up notification\n  - checking `structuredContent` against each tool's declared `outputSchema` (with a small built-in checker, so no new dependency) and confirming the text content matches it\n  - id echoing, error codes and malformed input\n- **`tests/test_tools.py`** gets a `DynamoStoreTest` class. It checks that results contain no Decimals and that results from the production store equal those from the fake store.\n\nOne judgement call for you: returning `\"\"` for notifications keeps `handle()` returning a string. Your HTTP layer needs to treat an empty string as \"send 202, no body\". If you'd rather return `None`, it's a one-line change."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3403,"cache_read_input_tokens":30552,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3403},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90240883-1d5a-4f9c-86a0-9af535729b1d","uuid":"06c42b16-d596-46be-85a7-b045e2cfaaa6","timestamp":"2026-10-04T01:55:09.219Z","request_id":"req_011CfgN5ShejDUeEwLyYcPFE"}
